course: religion-ethics
lesson: 78
title: Gifts of life: organ donation and giving blood
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes when a person dies, parts of their body that are still healthy, like the heart, kidneys, liver, lungs or the clear front of the eye, can be given to sick people who need them: this is organ donation, and Lily's teacher calls it «a gift of life».
explain: Ethan's uncle died in an accident, and his organs saved five people; so «part of him is still alive in other people», in a way, and his family can be proud of the gift he gave in a very sad week.
explain: There are never enough donors: Ollie hears that people needing a new kidney often wait for years, because the organ must match the sick person's body, a bit like a key that has to fit its lock.
explain: Giving blood is a gift living grown-ups can give: Sam's mum feels a quick pinch from the needle, and her blood can help people after accidents, in operations and with some illnesses.
explain: Most Christian churches call organ donation an act of love, following Jesus who gave himself for others, and many Muslim, Jewish, Hindu and Sikh leaders support it too; but it is a personal choice, some people say no for religious or personal reasons, and their choice is respected.
explain: For a child today this means: it is good to talk with your family about it, to say thank you for gifts of life, and to know that when you are older you can choose to help.
fact: Organ donation means giving healthy organs, such as the heart, kidneys, liver, lungs or corneas (the clear front of the eye), to people who need them, usually after the donor has died.
fact: One donor can save or improve the lives of several people; the NHS says up to nine.
fact: An organ must match the sick person's body, so people often wait a long time; in the UK many people wait two to three years for a kidney.
fact: Living people can give blood; in the UK you can start at 17.
fact: Giving blood feels like a quick pinch and does not hurt much, and the body soon makes new blood to replace it; the blood helps people after accidents, in operations and with illnesses.
fact: In England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland, adults are counted as willing donors unless they have said no, and families are always asked.
fact: Saying no to organ donation is a personal choice that is respected.
fact: Most Christian churches support organ donation as an act of love, and many leaders of other faiths support it too.
fact: Families of donors and people who received organs can write thank-you letters to each other through the hospital.
fact: In 2022 doctors in the USA were reported to have put a pig's heart into a man; using animal organs is very new, rare and still experimental.
fact: Some Muslims and Jews may feel unsure about pig organs because their faiths forbid eating pork, though many religious leaders say saving a life comes first.
fact: A person who gets a new heart can often live a healthy, active life afterwards.
exercise: What is organ donation? | Giving healthy organs to people who need them | Buying a new organ for a church | Giving away your old clothes to charity | Lending your body to a museum
exercise: Which of these can be donated? | A heart, kidneys or corneas | Your name and your address | Your memories and dreams | Your fingernails and hair colour
exercise: Ethan's uncle's organs saved five people. What does that mean? | His gift helped five people live | His ghost now lives in five people | Five people now share his memories | His family got five new relatives
exercise: How many lives does the NHS say one donor can help? | Up to nine | Only one | Exactly two | Over a hundred
exercise: Why do people sometimes wait years for a kidney? | There aren't enough donors, and it must match | Doctors make them wait for years as a test of how much they want it | Kidneys take years to grow in a jar | They have to save up to buy one
exercise: Mia asks if you can choose to be an organ donor. What is true in the UK? | Adults are counted in unless they say no, and families are asked | Nobody is ever allowed to choose | Only doctors can decide for you | Only children can sign up to donate
exercise: Lucy asks if it's okay to say no to donating. What is true? | Yes; it's a personal choice that is respected | No; saying no to donating is against the law in the UK | No; every religion forbids saying no | Yes, but only if you are very rich
exercise: Sam's mum gives blood. Does it hurt? | A quick pinch, not much more | It hurts more than a broken leg | It doesn't touch the skin at all | It hurts for weeks afterwards
exercise: What is donated blood used for? | Accidents, operations and some illnesses | Making medicine for poorly pets at the vet's | Painting the red crosses on hospital walls | Testing whether people in court are telling lies
exercise: From what age can you give blood in the UK? | 17 | 10 | 25 | 40
exercise: Holly's cousin had a heart transplant. What happened? | A donor's family gave the heart after the donor died | Doctors built her a heart from plastic bricks | She swapped hearts with her sister | She grew a new heart by eating vegetables
exercise: Can Ethan write a thank-you card to the donor's family? | Yes, through the hospital | No, it is forbidden by the hospital rules | Only on Christmas Day, with a present | Only if he already knows them very well
exercise: Freddie asks if animals can donate organs. What is true? | Pig hearts have been tried, but it's very new and rare | Every hospital uses pig hearts every day | Animal organs can never work in people | Only cats can give organs to people
exercise: Why might some Muslims and Jews feel unsure about pig organs? | Their faiths forbid eating pork | Pigs are too small to give organs | Pig organs are made of plastic | Their faiths forbid all medicine
exercise: How do most Christian churches see organ donation? | As an act of love for others | As something completely forbidden | As a job only for priests | As a way of buying heaven
exercise: Ruby's school holds a «Gift of Life» assembly. What can children do now? | Talk with their family about their wishes | Give some of their own blood at lunchtime in the hall | Sign hospital forms on their own, without telling anyone | Nothing; they must never talk about it with anyone at all
test: «So part of my uncle's still alive in other people? That's lowkey beautiful,» says Ethan (lowkey = quietly, really). Which is the most accurate answer? | In a way: his organs help others live | Yes, and he talks to them at night in their dreams | No, donated organs never really work in other people | Yes, they now have all of his memories as well
test: «Giving blood?? Nah, it's gonna kill me, it's mad scary,» says a boy in Leicester. What's true? | It's a quick pinch, and the body makes more blood | You can lose all of your blood in one go if the needle slips | Only doctors can give blood safely | It always hurts for weeks
test: The vicar announces, very formally: «Members of the congregation are encouraged to discuss with their families their wishes regarding organ donation.» What is he asking? | Families should talk about what each person would want | Everyone in the church must sign up to donate their organs today, after the service | The church will take everyone's organs | Nobody should ever talk about death
test: What would happen if many more people agreed to donate? | Fewer people would die waiting for organs | Hospitals would close because nobody is sick | Organs would stop matching anyone | Nothing; donors never really help
test: «Why don't they just get a kidney from the shop, innit?» asks a kid in Croydon. What's true? | Kidneys come from donors and must match; there aren't enough | Kidneys are sold in big chemist shops in every town, next to the plasters | Doctors grow kidneys in a few hours | Kidneys can be made from any animal
test: «Saying no to donating is so selfish, frfr,» says a teen in Atlanta (frfr = for real, for real). What's the fair answer? | It's a personal choice, and people's reasons are respected | She's right; saying no is a crime that people can go to prison for | She's right; all faiths forbid saying no | Only selfish people have any reasons
test: «Can a pig give a heart? That's well weird,» asks Freddie. What's true? | It has been tried, but it's very new and still experimental | Yes, it happens every day in every hospital in the country, with pig hearts | No, animal organs could never be tried | Only to other pigs, never to people
test: In a cartoon, a robot swaps its heart for a new one in five seconds. What's real about heart transplants? | A real heart comes from a donor and needs a long operation | Real hearts are swapped in five seconds | Real hearts are built by robots | Real hearts can be bought online
test: «Holly's cousin's got a new heart and she's playin' footy again, bonza!» says an Aussie uncle (bonza = great). What does this show? | A transplant can give someone a healthy, active life | Transplants always make people run faster than before | Football is safe only after a transplant | The heart came from a famous footballer, so she plays like him now
test: «When I'm big I'm gonna give blood every time I can,» says Ruby. When can she start in the UK? | At 17 | At 9 | At 30 | Never
test: Myth to check: «Most religions forbid organ donation.» | False; most faith leaders support it, though people choose | True; nearly every religion in the world forbids it completely and always has | True, but only Christianity allows it | False; religions have never discussed it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises organ donation? | The gift of healthy organs, usually after death, to persons in need of them | The sale of organs to the highest bidder | The removal of organs from living persons without consent | The preservation of organs in museums for display
exam: Identify the minimum age at which a person may first donate blood in the United Kingdom. | Seventeen years | Ten years | Twenty-five years | Forty years
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the law of organ donation in the United Kingdom. | Adults are deemed willing to donate unless they have declined, and families are consulted | Donation is compulsory for all persons without exception | Donation is prohibited by law throughout the United Kingdom | Only children may register a decision to donate
exam: Whereas a person declines to become an organ donor, ascertain the response most consistent with the matters taught. | The decision is a personal one and ought to be respected | The decision constitutes a criminal offence punishable under the law | The decision is forbidden by every faith | The decision must be overruled by the family
